%T A396252 21960,30384,147492,26208,419976,5040,32004,1333728,766728,1872,99540,
%U A396252 2250144,15624,708624,790308,390240,1944072,19152,131076,5175072,2829960,57168,1679652,7294176,438984
%N A396252 Conductor of the elliptic curve y^2 = x^3 + 3*n*x + (1 - n^3), the Kraus-Frey curve for x^3 + y^3 = z^k, at (x,y) = (n,1).
%C A396252 E_{n,1}: y^2 = x^3 + 3*n*x + (1-n^3) is the Frey-Hellegouarch (Kraus) curve used in the modular method for x^3 + y^3 = z^k. Its discriminant is -432*(n^3+1)^2.
%C A396252 The 2-adic valuation of a(n) depends only on n mod 4.
%H A396252 N. Freitas, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4171/cmh/386">On the Fermat-type equation x^3 + y^3 = z^p</a>, Commentarii Mathematici Helvetici, 91 (2016), 295-304.
%H A396252 A. Kraus, <a href="https://projecteuclid.org/journals/experimental-mathematics/volume-7/issue-1/Sur-l%c3%a9quation-asp-3bsp-3csp-p/em/1047674269.full">Sur l'équation a^3 + b^3 = c^p</a>, Experimental Mathematics, 7 (1998), 1-13.
%F A396252 a(n) = 2^c(n) * 3^2 * r5(n^3+1), where c(n) = 5, 3, 4, 2 for n == 1, 2, 3, 0 (mod 4) (period 4), and r5(m) is the product of the distinct primes >= 5 dividing m. Thus 3^2 exactly divides a(n) for all n, and the part of a(n) coprime to 6 equals r5(n^3+1) and is squarefree. Proved by Tate's algorithm on the curve.
%e A396252 a(2) = 72: for n=2 the curve is y^2 = x^3 + 6*x - 7, with conductor 72 = 2^3 * 3^2 and r5(2^3+1) = r5(9) = 1.
%o A396252 (PARI) a(n) = ellglobalred(ellinit([0,0,0,3*n,1-n^3]))[1]
%o A396252 (PARI) r5(m) = my(f=factor(m)); prod(i=1, #f~, if(f[i,1]>=5, f[i,1], 1));
%o A396252 a(n) = 2^[5,3,4,2][((n-1)%4)+1] * 9 * r5(n^3+1)
%Y A396252 Cf. A099426.
%K A396252 nonn,easy
%O A396252 1,1
